From 7a48cadd067f4078a601ee5ea221df8521665a53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomasz Staniak <68373859+tomaszstaniak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 11:42:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/11] Stop compiling the exec glue against a different SDK
 than we link with
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

csrc/execglue.c reaches exec.library through proto/exec.h, and how that inline
passes SysBase is decided by the SDK the headers came from. AROS One 1.3 uses a
register call — mov %rax,%r12; call *-0x108(%rax). Mainline passes SysBase as
an ordinary third argument — mov %rax,%rdx; jmp *-0x108(%rdx). The two are not
interchangeable.

build.rs was mixing them. It tried to read the toolchain out of the target
spec, on the assumption that TARGET holds a path ending in .json; cargo passes
the target name, so that branch never fired and AROS_GCC/AROS_SDK from the
environment quietly decided the compiler. Linking against mainline while
AROS_SDK still pointed at AROS One produced a binary that printed its greeting,
allocated on the heap, and then died inside AllocMem with a PC in a Kickstart
module — which reads like a broken startup or an uninitialised SysBase, and is
neither.

build.rs now requires AROS_GCC and AROS_SDK, reruns when either changes, and
says what they mean if they are missing. setup.sh writes both, and additionally
detects how the SDK splits its C library: AROS One puts everything in libcrt.a,
which gcc links by default, while mainline needs -lposixc -lstdcio -lstdc
-lautoinit or the link ends with three or four undefined symbols. It prints
which profile it chose.

Verified on mainline AROS: the template now builds with a plain `cargo run`
and prints all of its output, including the exec.library allocation that used
to crash. TARGET-NOTES records the ABI difference, the SDK layouts, and what
has actually been run on which build.
